The surest way to trust a protocol is to watch it run. pancake ships runnable
examples at two levels: short Zig chapters that peel the library one layer at a
time, and full cross-language apps where a coordinator in one language talks to
clients in four others over real [OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn). Every one
compiles and runs under the build, so the code on the page cannot drift from the
library.

## The Zig chapters

They read top-down. Chapter 01 is the surface an application rides; each later
chapter peels one layer beneath it, ending at the raw engine.

| # | Chapter | What it shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 01 | [connect & serve](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig) | the application surface over a real TCP socket: listen, accept, publish by name, serve; connect, fetch, call, switch on the outcome |
| 02 | [actor far-send](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/02_actor_far_send.zig) | the `Vat` pattern beneath a call: publish an actor, far-send to it, settle the promise |
| 03 | [look up by name](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/03_look_up_by_name.zig) | the bootstrap fetch by swiss number that sits under fetch-by-name |
| 04 | [call a remote method](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/04_call_a_remote_method.zig) | the raw engine: pair two peers and shuttle deliver and resolve one `step` at a time |

`zig build examples` builds and runs them all, and they also run under
`zig build check`. The
[example chapters](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
carry the full source and a few lower-level mechanics besides.

## The cross-language apps

Each app is built on the same small surface (connect, fetch, publish, spawn, send,
serve, close), written once per language, so the app reads the same in Zig and the
four [bindings]($link.page('bindings')): Rust, Python, Racket, and TypeScript.

| App | What it is | Run it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [rooms](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/) | a capability-secure chat room, real-time | `zig build demo-rooms` |
| [rooms over iroh](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/) | the same chat over encrypted QUIC | `zig build demo-rooms-iroh` (needs cargo) |
| [tasks](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/tasks/) | a shared task board: CRUD plus live state | `zig build demo-tasks` |
| [tools](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/tools/) | a capability showcase for AI agent harnesses: delegation, attenuation, revocation | `zig build demo-tools` |
| [accountability](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/accountability-on-pancake/) | a signed-call accountability app over a real OCapN/TCP session | `zig build demo-accountability` |

The `rooms`, `tasks`, and `tools` demos need only `deno` and the always-built Zig
client; the Python, Racket, and Rust clients join when their runtimes are present
and are skipped with a note otherwise. The `accountability` demo pairs a Zig host
with Deno and Python callers. Each binds an ephemeral port by default; pin it with
`-Dport`, or add `-Dattach=true` to join a server you already started. They run as
siblings of `check`, not as part of the hermetic gate.

The `rooms over iroh` variant is the one to watch. It runs the same five-language
chat, but the coordinator and clients reach each other over an encrypted
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) channel instead of a local TCP port: each peer
is addressed by a self-authenticating node id, and the bytes on the wire are keyed
QUIC. It is the clearest demonstration that the encrypted, peer-to-peer transport is
a drop-in under the same application surface, the app code does not change, only the
address does. It needs `cargo` for the iroh sidecar.

Why an application at all? A protocol can pass every conformance scenario and still
be miserable to build on. These apps are where the surface meets a real program, in
five languages at once, and where the rough edges no fixture reaches show up.
